---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
import Bookmarklet from '../components/Bookmarklet.astro'
import ContrastChecker from '../components/ContrastChecker.astro'
import Command from '../components/Command.astro'
import { blob, breadcrumb, install, site } from '../lib/site'

const description =
  'A WCAG contrast checker that composites translucent colours the way a browser does, a bookmarklet that runs loupe’s measurement script on any page, and a CLI that runs loupe’s measuring in CI.'
---

<Base title="Tools" description={description} path="/tools" card="tools" schema={[breadcrumb(['Tools', '/tools'])]}>
  <header class="page-head">
    <h1>Measure it yourself</h1>
    <p class="lede">Three pieces of loupe that work without Claude. The first two run in your browser and send nothing anywhere; the third runs on your machine or in CI.</p>
  </header>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="contrast-h">
    <header>
      <h2 id="contrast-h">Contrast checker</h2>
      <p>
        Type any colour your browser understands, including <code>oklch()</code> and colours with transparency. The
        foreground is drawn over the background before measuring, as on a page. The ratio is cut, not rounded, to two
        places, so 4.499 shows as 4.49 and fails.
      </p>
    </header>
    <ContrastChecker />
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="bm-h">
    <header>
      <h2 id="bm-h">Measure any page</h2>
      <p>
        Add it as a bookmark, open any page, and click the bookmark. It runs
        {' '}<a href={blob('scripts/measure.js')}>scripts/measure.js</a>, the script loupe runs during a review, on the page
        as you see it now, and opens a panel with the results: text that fails contrast, targets under 24 pixels, type
        sizes, motion that ignores reduced motion, and template defaults. Press Escape to close it.
      </p>
    </header>
    <Bookmarklet />
    <p class="hint">
      It measures one width, one colour scheme and one motion setting: whatever your browser has now. A review measures
      twelve combinations. Some sites block bookmarklets with a Content Security Policy. If nothing happens when you
      click it, that is the likely reason; the browser console shows the block.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="cli-h">
    <header>
      <h2 id="cli-h">Measure in CI</h2>
      <p>
        <a href={blob('bin/loupe.mjs')}>bin/loupe.mjs</a> runs the three scripts a review runs on a web page, with no model: the
        measurement at three widths, light and dark, with and without reduced motion; a keyboard walk with real Tab
        presses; and the stress modes (a 320-pixel window, wider text spacing, 200% text, translated and right-to-left
        text). It works in Chromium, WebKit and Firefox and prints a markdown summary or JSON.
      </p>
    </header>
    <Command text={install.cli} label="CLI commands" />
    <p>
      Only measured problems that no WCAG exception covers fail the run: text contrast, targets under 24 pixels without
      the spacing exception, a focusable control with no name, a keyboard trap outside a dialog, and focus hidden
      entirely under a fixed bar. Everything that needs judgement, such as a missing focus style or text that clips
      under stress, is a warning. A review rates the same rows by its own rules and confirms the warnings first.
    </p>
    <p>
      With <code>--baseline</code> it compares the run with a committed file and fails only on new findings. Finding ids
      come from the rule, the role, the text and the colours rather than the element’s position, so adding a list item
      doesn’t turn one finding into a new one and a fixed one. Write the baseline on the same machine image as the check:
      fonts change text widths. The <a href={blob('README.md#the-cli')}>README</a> has a GitHub Actions workflow.
    </p>
    <p class="hint">
      Version {site.version}. Point it only at pages you control, and keep secrets out of the job: the browser runs whatever
      the page runs.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
